**Dual-write (phase 1).** Legacy `pg-orders-1` stays the source of truth for every read and write. Each write also commits an `orders_outbox` row in the same transaction, and `outbox-relay` applies those rows to orders-v2 asynchronously. `backfill_orders_v2` copies historical orders each night. Nothing reads orders-v2 yet, so its errors can't reach customers. The phase ends only when four things hold:

- the backfill is complete;
- the `order-diff` mismatch rate stays under 0.01% for 3 days;
- relay lag p99 stays under 2 seconds for the same 3 days;
- a one-time full-table checksum matches.

**Cutover (phase 2).** Reads move first. `orders.read_from_v2` ramps through 1%, 10%, 50% and 100%, one step a day, while both stores still receive writes, and then holds at 100% for a 48-hour soak. Until this point, rollback just means setting the flag back to 0, and nothing is lost. Turning off `orders.write_legacy` is the point of no return, because nothing syncs back to legacy. That step therefore needs sign-off from the DBA lead and the commerce director. Legacy then stays up read-only for 30 days before it is decommissioned.

**The timeline has no slack.** The four ramp days and the two soak days end exactly on 2026-11-30, the day before the code freeze. If any ramp step has to hold, the write stop slips to January and dual-write keeps running through the freeze.
